# Email Trigger

## Overview

Use an Email Trigger when you want inbound emails to run a project agent.

A typical setup is:

1. Resend receives an inbound email.
2. Resend sends a webhook event to your backend.
3. Your backend fetches or reads the email content.
4. Your backend calls the project API trigger.
5. The connected agent receives the email as a message.

## Prerequisites

You need:

- A project API key
- An API trigger card connected to the agent
- A backend endpoint that can receive Resend webhooks
- A Resend inbound route or webhook

Store the project API key in your backend environment:

```bash
RADDUS_CANVAS_API_KEY=your_project_api_key
```

## 1. Create the API trigger

In the project canvas:

1. Create or select the agent that should process inbound emails.
2. Add an API trigger card.
3. Connect the API trigger card to the agent.
4. Create a project API key.
5. Store the API key in your backend as `RADDUS_CANVAS_API_KEY`.

## 2. Add a Resend webhook endpoint

Create a backend route that receives Resend webhook events.

```ts
import express from "express";
import { Resend } from "resend";

const app = express();
const resend = new Resend(process.env.RESEND_API_KEY);

app.use(express.json());

app.post("/webhooks/resend", async (req, res) => {
  const event = req.body;

  if (event.type !== "email.received") {
    return res.sendStatus(204);
  }

  const email = await resend.emails.get(event.data.email_id);
  await runAgentFromEmail(email);

  res.sendStatus(200);
});
```

## 3. Forward the email to the API trigger

Call the project API trigger from your backend.

```ts
async function runAgentFromEmail(email: {
  from: string;
  to?: string[];
  subject: string;
  text?: string;
  html?: string;
}) {
  const response = await fetch("https://your-raddus-api.example.com/api/run", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: {
      authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.RADDUS_CANVAS_API_KEY}`,
      "content-type": "application/json",
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      message: [
        "Inbound email received",
        `From: ${email.from}`,
        email.to?.length ? `To: ${email.to.join(", ")}` : "",
        `Subject: ${email.subject}`,
        "",
        email.text || email.html || "",
      ].filter(Boolean).join("\n"),
    }),
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    const body = await response.text();
    throw new Error(`API trigger failed: ${response.status} ${body}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}
```

## 4. Configure Resend

In Resend:

1. Add or verify the receiving domain.
2. Configure inbound routing.
3. Set the webhook URL to your backend route, for example:

```text
https://your-backend.example.com/webhooks/resend
```

4. Send a test email to the inbound address.
5. Confirm your backend receives the webhook.
6. Confirm the project agent runs from the API trigger.

## 5. Recommended production checks

Before using this in production:

- Verify Resend webhook signatures if enabled for your setup.
- Reject unsupported event types.
- Ignore duplicate webhook deliveries.
- Limit accepted recipient addresses.
- Log the API trigger response status.
- Avoid forwarding untrusted attachments directly into the agent.
- Strip or sanitize very large HTML bodies.

# Slack Trigger

## Overview

Use a Slack Trigger when you want Slack messages to run a project agent.

A typical setup is:

1. Slack sends an event to your backend.
2. Your backend verifies the Slack request signature.
3. Your backend filters the event.
4. Your backend calls the project API trigger.
5. The connected agent receives the Slack message as a prompt.

## Prerequisites

You need:

- A project API key
- An API trigger card connected to the agent
- A Slack app with a bot user
- A backend endpoint that can receive Slack Events API requests

Store secrets in your backend environment:

```bash
RADDUS_CANVAS_API_KEY=your_project_api_key
SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=your_slack_signing_secret
```

## 1. Create the API trigger

In the project canvas:

1. Create or select the agent that should process Slack messages.
2. Add an API trigger card.
3. Connect the API trigger card to the agent.
4. Create a project API key.
5. Store the API key in your backend as `RADDUS_CANVAS_API_KEY`.

## 2. Create a Slack event endpoint

Slack sends Events API requests to your backend. Your endpoint should handle URL verification and message events.

```ts
import express from "express";

const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

app.post("/webhooks/slack", async (req, res) => {
  const body = req.body;

  if (body.type === "url_verification") {
    return res.json({ challenge: body.challenge });
  }

  const event = body.event;

  if (event?.type !== "message" || event.bot_id) {
    return res.sendStatus(204);
  }

  await runAgentFromSlack(event);

  res.sendStatus(200);
});
```

## 3. Verify Slack signatures

In production, verify Slack request signatures before trusting the event.

Slack signs requests with `SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET`.

```ts
import crypto from "crypto";

function verifySlackSignature(req: express.Request, rawBody: string) {
  const timestamp = req.header("x-slack-request-timestamp");
  const signature = req.header("x-slack-signature");

  if (!timestamp || !signature) {
    throw new Error("Missing Slack signature headers");
  }

  const age = Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - Number(timestamp));
  if (age > 60 * 5) {
    throw new Error("Slack request timestamp is too old");
  }

  const base = `v0:${timestamp}:${rawBody}`;
  const digest = crypto
    .createHmac("sha256", process.env.SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET!)
    .update(base)
    .digest("hex");

  const expected = `v0=${digest}`;

  if (!crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(expected), Buffer.from(signature))) {
    throw new Error("Invalid Slack signature");
  }
}
```

To verify signatures correctly, preserve the raw request body:

```ts
app.use(express.json({
  verify: (req, _res, buf) => {
    (req as any).rawBody = buf.toString("utf8");
  },
}));
```

## 4. Forward the Slack message to the API trigger

Call the project API trigger from your backend.

```ts
async function runAgentFromSlack(event: {
  channel: string;
  user: string;
  text: string;
  ts?: string;
  thread_ts?: string;
}) {
  const response = await fetch("https://your-raddus-api.example.com/api/run", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: {
      authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.RADDUS_CANVAS_API_KEY}`,
      "content-type": "application/json",
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      message: [
        "Slack message received",
        `Channel: ${event.channel}`,
        `User: ${event.user}`,
        event.thread_ts ? `Thread: ${event.thread_ts}` : "",
        event.ts ? `Timestamp: ${event.ts}` : "",
        "",
        event.text,
      ].filter(Boolean).join("\n"),
    }),
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    const body = await response.text();
    throw new Error(`API trigger failed: ${response.status} ${body}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}
```

## 5. Configure the Slack app

In Slack app settings:

1. Enable Event Subscriptions.
2. Set the request URL to your backend route, for example:

```text
https://your-backend.example.com/webhooks/slack
```

3. Subscribe to bot events such as:

```text
message.channels
message.groups
app_mention
```

4. Add the required OAuth scopes, such as:

```text
channels:history
groups:history
app_mentions:read
```

5. Install or reinstall the app in the workspace.
6. Invite the bot to the channels it should monitor.

## 6. Recommended production checks

Before using this in production:

- Verify Slack request signatures.
- Handle Slack URL verification.
- Ignore bot messages.
- Ignore unsupported event types.
- Deduplicate repeated Slack event IDs.
- Filter channels or users if only some messages should run the agent.
- Log the API trigger response status.
- Return quickly to Slack and process long work asynchronously if needed.
